pany called novo nordisk which is one  of the top three suppliers of insulin in  the country  and it's going to be quality i mean the  regulations now on generic drugs are  very good  walmart sells several hundred drugs for  four dollars  with no insurance cost a month so  these are quality drugs and they can  fight most diseases that we all face  so lisa what impact could walmart's move  have on this industry  oh it it it really could be huge and you  know they sort of  they're they're they've been a long time  leader in offering  low-cost supplies and as well as the  4.10  drugs to americans for a really long  time  hopefully here it will put sort of  continued pressure on pharmaceutical  companies to  keep those really great sort of of  pocket  discount programs coupon programs and  patient assistant programs  low and affordable so i would say the  more attention that is brought to this  issue  the the more that people realize that  they don't really need to pay more than  100 bucks or sometimes even 35 dollars  out of pocket  for their insulin it's a it's a very  very good thing hey dr agus  insulin in america costs up to 10 times  more than it does in other countries why  is that you know  it's a great question and certainly we  have a runaway train it's a predatory  practice it costs 10 times more because  they will be paid 10 times more and  that's not right you know when banting  identified insulin in 1923 he declined  to put his name on the patent  because he didn't think it right to make  a profit on something like insulin that  would save people's lives  so we have to go back toward value-based  pricing  in our country because otherwise it's  not sustainable  lisa do you think this is likely to have  an impact on the price of insulin  overall  um you know we we would hope so i mean  we we've already  some of my investigations have shown  that surprisingly  after the three big drug makers that  make insulin  were called before congress about two  years ago